// A brace initializer with values on a LOCAL PRIMITIVE-typed declaration ('int x {5};' /
// 'int x = {5};') is not struct field-init and not a recognized container brace-init,
// so it used to fall into EmitFieldInitializer's generic struct-field-init failure
// message ("'int' is not a known struct type") - technically true but describing a
// construct the user did not write. Found while routing the bare-brace spelling
// ('int x {5};', no '=') through the same local declarator handling the '=' spelling
// already used: PRE, the bare form silently read an uninitialized garbage value (rc 0,
// e.g. "x=1876644176"); this reject is new and intentional for that spelling, and the
// message must name the real situation for BOTH spellings rather than misdescribe it
// as a struct problem (the '=' spelling already rejected before this fix, just with
// the same misleading wording).
// Not fixed here: making 'int x {5};' actually assign 5 - that is a language feature
// (primitive brace-value-init), out of scope for this bug fix; file separately if wanted.
// (A GLOBAL primitive brace-init like 'int x = {5};' at file scope is a different,
// pre-existing silent-zero gap - unaffected by this fix, not covered by this leg.)
extern int main()
{
    expect_error("brace initializer with values is not supported on 'x' - 'int' is not a struct/union/class or a recognized container") {
        int x = { 5 };
    }
    expect_error("brace initializer with values is not supported on 'x' - 'int' is not a struct/union/class or a recognized container") {
        int x { 5 };
    }
    return 0;
}
